Q: Does Laragon ignore .htaccess? A: <p>For those still looking for an answer, <strong>Laragon does not ignore .htaccess</strong></p>

<p>The error was a simple syntax error in the <code>IfModule</code></p>

<pre><code>&lt;IfModule mod_rewrite.c | rewrite_module&gt;
    # Logic
&lt;/IfModule&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: How do I place a login screen inside a fragment? A: <p><strong>I also want do like this but after my research i decide it best to use our layout directly in fragment rather than call activity object in fragment and load activity code in perticular fragment.</strong></p>

<p>Edit your LoginFragment :</p>

<pre><code>public class LoginFragment extends Fragment {

    public LoginFragment() {
        // Required empty public constructor
    }

    @Override
    public void onCreate(Bundle savedInstanceState) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState);



    }
    @Override
    public View onCreateView(LayoutInflater inflater, ViewGroup container,
                             Bundle savedInstanceState) {
        View rootView = inflater.inflate(R.layout.login, container, false);


        // Inflate the layout for this fragment

TextView registerScreen = (TextView) rootView.findViewById(R.id.link_to_register);

        // Listening to register new account link
        registerScreen.setOnClickListener(new View.OnClickListener() {

            public void onClick(View v) {
                // Switching to Register screen
                Intent i = new Intent(getApplicationContext(), RegisterActivity.class);
                startActivity(i);
            }
        });

        return rootView;
    }

    @Override
    public void onAttach(Activity activity) {
        super.onAttach(activity);
    }

    @Override
    public void onDetach() {
        super.onDetach();
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Android camera preview callback and mediarecorder record video A: <p>Solution a:
You can change method startrecording() to:</p>

<pre><code>    private void startrecording(){

    mCamera = getCameraInstance();
    mMediaRecorder = new MediaRecorder();

    // Step 1: Unlock and set camera to MediaRecorder
    mCamera.unlock();
    mMediaRecorder.setCamera(mCamera);

    // Step 2: Set sources
    mMediaRecorder.setAudioSource(MediaRecorder.AudioSource.CAMCORDER);
    mMediaRecorder.setVideoSource(MediaRecorder.VideoSource.CAMERA);

    // Step 3: Set a CamcorderProfile (requires API Level 8 or higher)
    mMediaRecorder.setProfile(CamcorderProfile.get(CamcorderProfile.QUALITY_HIGH));

    // Step 4: Set output file
    mMediaRecorder.setOutputFile(getOutputMediaFile(MEDIA_TYPE_VIDEO).toString());

    // Step 5: Set the preview output
    mMediaRecorder.setPreviewDisplay(mPreview.getHolder().getSurface());

    // Step 6: Prepare configured MediaRecorder
    try {
        mMediaRecorder.prepare();
    } catch (IllegalStateException e) {
        Log.d(TAG, "IllegalStateException preparing MediaRecorder: " + e.getMessage());
        releaseMediaRecorder();
        return false;
    } catch (IOException e) {
        Log.d(TAG, "IOException preparing MediaRecorder: " + e.getMessage());
        releaseMediaRecorder();

    }
    mMediaRecorder.start();
}
</code></pre>

Q: get the primary key index_type in MySQL A: <p>For an InnoDB's PRIMARY KEY, these are probably all the choices you get:</p>
<pre><code>index_option: {
  | BTREE             -- optional and no choice
  | COMMENT 'string'  -- optional
}
</code></pre>

Q: Alternative way to check if input value is present in `enum` or not. Currently working with `std::ranges::contains` and `std::array` A: <p>Experimental snippet as requested in comments, using C++26 reflection:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include&lt;meta&gt;
#include&lt;ranges&gt;

template &lt;typename E&gt;
requires(std::meta::is_enum_type(^^E))
constexpr auto enum_meta
=[]{
 return std::define_static_array
      ( std::meta::enumerators_of(^^E)
      | std::views::transform
        ([](auto em){
           return std::pair
                { std::meta::identifier_of(em)
                , [:em:] };
        }) );
}();
</code></pre>
<p>The above template can be evaluated for any given <code>enum</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>constexpr auto FileTypeArr
= enum_meta&lt;FileType&gt;;

bool test = std::ranges::contains
( FileTypeArr, 3
, [](auto p)
  { return std::to_underlying(p.first); } );
</code></pre>
<p>The problem is that C++26 reflection is too fresh already, and not fully supported yet. Thus we need an adhoc alternative. Luckily, <code>&lt;source_location&gt;</code> is C++20:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;source_location&gt;

template&lt;auto v&gt;
consteval std::string_view mangled()
{ return std::source_location
       ::current().function_name(); };

consteval std::string_view demangle(std::string_view);
</code></pre>
<p><code>std::source_location</code> is a portable replacement for file/line/function macros. But the <code>mangled</code> function results in a platform specific decoration of value <code>v</code>. That decorated value needs <code>demangle</code>ing before use. The details of demangling can be determined by running <code>mangle</code> on a few enumeration values on the target platform; so I skip the details here.
For small enough enumerations, it's possible to craft <code>enum_meta</code> array:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;ranges&gt;
#include &lt;type_traits&gt;

constexpr std::size_t ICE_limit = 4 *1024;

template&lt;typename E&gt;
requires std::is_enum_v&lt;E&gt;
constexpr auto enum_meta
=[]&lt;std::size_t...Is&gt;
(std::index_sequence&lt;Is...&gt;){
    constexpr static auto large_list
    = std::array
    { std::pair
      { demangle(mangle&lt;E(Is)&gt;())
      , E(Is) } ... }
    | std::views::filter(
      [](auto p){
         auto i = std::to_underlying
                  (p.first.front());
         return (p &gt; '9')
             or (p &lt; '0');
      });

    std::array
    &lt; std::pair&lt;std::string_view, E&gt;
    , std::ranges::distance(large_list) &gt; 
    ret;

    std::ranges::copy(large_list, begin(ret));
    return ret;

}(std::make_index_sequence&lt;ICE_limit&gt;{});
</code></pre>
<p>For large values of <code>ICE_limit</code>, compiler fails to compile <code>std::make_index_sequence</code>. On MSVC, that limit is below 8K. Therefore, this approach is limited to small enumerations only. Moreover, I have only considered unsigned enumerations with explicitly specified underlying types, or <code>enum class</code>es. Old school enumerations probably fail to compile, and signed enumerations require more work. In comparison, the C++26 reflection takes much less effort, if available.</p>
